WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.120
He wanted to be the very best like no one ever was&nbsp;
— and it only took him a couple decades to do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.680
Ash Ketchum’s career as a Pokémon trainer&nbsp;
has been more shocking than his Pikachu.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.840
In anime, it's not unusual for young male&nbsp;
protagonists to be voiced by women. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.580
the case in popular anime like "Naruto,"&nbsp;
"One Piece," "Hunter x Hunter," and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.120
of course, "Pokémon." In both the&nbsp;
Japanese and English versions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:29.280
Ash is voiced by a few different women. He's&nbsp;
played by Rica Matsumoto in the Japanese version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.620
while Veronica Taylor, Sarah Natochenny, and&nbsp;
Kayzie Rogers have voiced him in English dubs.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.620
"I’m Ash Ketchum! I came from Pallet Town in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.420
Kanto region! And this is my good&nbsp;
buddy Pikachu. Nice to meetcha!"

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.640
In addition to voicing Ash, Taylor and&nbsp;
Natochenny have also voiced his mother, Delia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.500
along with other characters and Pokémon. That's&nbsp;
an impressive feat, considering how differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.300
the two characters sound. The other characters&nbsp;
Taylor and Natochenny have voiced — like Ash's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:03.120
traveling companion May and the gym leader Skyla,&nbsp;
respectively — also sound nothing like him. Rogers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.940
voiced Ash in 2006's "The Mastermind of Mirage&nbsp;
Pokémon" special, but has also provided voices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:14.100
for a number of Pokémon with their own distinct&nbsp;
cries — like Mr. Mime and Jessie’s Wobbuffet.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.840
Since the "Pokémon" anime is based on the&nbsp;
"Pokémon" video game series, it makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.100
that Ash has a lot in common with the protagonists&nbsp;
from the games. In every generation of the video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.600
game series, the player characters receive their&nbsp;
starter Pokémon from the professor of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.060
region, battle their rivals, and share the same&nbsp;
goal of becoming the Pokémon champion. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.220
also the case for Ash, who gets his Pikachu from&nbsp;
Professor Oak, battles it out with rivals like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.540
Gary and Paul, and participates in tournaments&nbsp;
with the championship title on the line.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.280
Perhaps the most specific similarity between&nbsp;
Ash and the video game protagonists is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.960
with one exception, they don't have a father&nbsp;
figure around. Just as Ash always has his mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.520
Delia to come home to, the video game protagonists&nbsp;
can always visit their mothers in their hometown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.320
However, it remains a mystery where all of&nbsp;
the fathers are. The only exception is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.300
the third-generation games where the player's&nbsp;
father is the Petalburg gym leader, Norman.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.140
One of the most memorable parts of the&nbsp;
"Pokémon" anime comes when Ash meets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.160
Misty in the very first episode. While being&nbsp;
attacked and chased by a flock of Spearow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.040
Ash rushes to get Pikachu and himself to safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.060
and gets caught on Misty's fishing line.&nbsp;
Instead of properly introducing himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.761
Ash hops onto Misty's bike without permission&nbsp;
and speeds off toward the nearest Pokémon Center.

00:02:26.761 --> 00:02:30.548
"Hey, what are you doing?!"
"I’m borrowing this."&nbsp;

00:02:30.548 --> 00:02:35.585
"Wait, hey, that’s my bike!"
"I’ll give it back someday!"

00:02:35.585 --> 00:02:39.480
Spoiler alert: the bike never gets&nbsp;
properly returned. Pikachu ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.080
destroying it while attacking the Spearow&nbsp;
flock with a powerful electric move.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.820
Unfortunately for bike owners in the Pokémon&nbsp;
universe, this is not a one-time thing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:52.200
Ash and Pikachu: the same thing that&nbsp;
happens to Misty also happens to several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.400
of Ash’s other friends. In the first&nbsp;
episode of "Pokémon Advanced," Pikachu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.540
zaps May's bike along with Team Rocket.&nbsp;
Likewise, the second episode of "Pokémon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.800
Diamond and Pearl" sees the destruction of&nbsp;
Dawn's bike during a battle with Pikachu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.420
If it has two wheels and pedals, it probably&nbsp;
won’t last a day with Ash and Pikachu around.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.400
"Hey, what happened to your bike?"&nbsp;

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.900
"What happened to my bike?! You&nbsp;
happened to my bike, you little loser!"

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.180
As someone who dreams of being the very best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.740
Ash must face off against the toughest&nbsp;
Pokémon trainers in every region. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.120
might be easy enough to do in the video games&nbsp;
with enough experience points, but it's no easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.280
task in the anime. Between gym battles and&nbsp;
tournaments, Ash actually loses quite a bit.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.740
In fact, Ash loses his very first gym battle.&nbsp;
In Episode 5, the young trainer rushes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.080
the Pewter City Gym and challenges Brock to&nbsp;
a battle without proper preparation. Brock's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.960
Onix wraps its massive stone body around Pikachu&nbsp;
and immediately forces Ash to surrender. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.120
in Episode 14, Ash and Pikachu suffer&nbsp;
a demoralizing loss to Lt. Surge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.080
his Raichu that almost convinces the&nbsp;
10-year-old to evolve his companion.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.520
The most humiliating defeat, however, comes&nbsp;
during the hyped league battle between Ash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.140
and his friendly rival Ritchie at the&nbsp;
end of the Indigo League arc. Ash puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.460
his faith in his powerful yet disobedient&nbsp;
Charizard to defeat Ritchie's Pikachu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.240
but Charizard lies down and&nbsp;
refuses to obey. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.698
Ash loses the match and is eliminated from&nbsp;
the Indigo Plateau Conference entirely.

00:04:20.698 --> 00:04:23.760
In addition to losing a few gym&nbsp;
battles in the Kanto Region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.960
Ash also acquires some of his gym badges&nbsp;
in ways that don't really seem fair to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.520
other trainers. The fact that this happens more&nbsp;
than once throughout Ash's Kanto journey makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.020
us question his true talents as a trainer&nbsp;
and takes away from his early achievements.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.820
After Ash loses to Brock for the first time,&nbsp;
he trains up his Pikachu and challenges the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.380
rock-type gym leader to a rematch. This time,&nbsp;
Pikachu defeats Brock's rock and ground-type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.640
Geodude with a single electric attack —&nbsp;
which should technically not be possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.080
since ground-types are typically immune to&nbsp;
electric attacks. When Onix is on the field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.240
the sprinklers conveniently go off and weaken&nbsp;
Onix to the point where Pikachu's attack is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.920
enough to bring it down. Ash ends up showing&nbsp;
mercy to the Onix and abandons the match,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.100
earning him the Boulder Badge&nbsp;
along with Brock’s respect.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:13.740
Another unconventional gym victory happens with&nbsp;
Ash’s rematch against Sabrina and her Kadabra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.680
in Episode 24. Just like their initial fight,&nbsp;
Sabrina seems to be in full control. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.060
the battle is interrupted by the mischievous&nbsp;
Haunter who shows up after following Ash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:29.220
from Lavender Town. The Haunter goofs around,&nbsp;
making Sabrina and her Kadabra laugh until they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:34.500
are deemed unable to battle. By default, Ash is&nbsp;
declared the winner and receives the Marsh Badge.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.820
"Maybe you should just quit now. Besides,&nbsp;
the badges you do have, you got out of pity!"&nbsp;

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.500
"A badge is a badge!"
"Ow!"

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.620
Since the beginning of his journey, Ash&nbsp;
has captured many different Pokémon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.000
some of which turn out to be incredibly&nbsp;
strong members of his team with a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.160
bit of training. The best example of this is&nbsp;
Ash’s first fire-type: caught as a Charmander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.320
it evolves into Charmeleon and then Charizard,&nbsp;
and it receives special training at Charicific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.580
Valley. To this day, Charizard is still&nbsp;
considered one of Ash's strongest Pokémon.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:10.980
Unfortunately, many of Ash's other Pokémon don't&nbsp;
get the same amount of attention or training to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.600
become that powerful. This is most evident in the&nbsp;
fact that Ash has a lot of Pokémon that aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.720
fully evolved, including Totodile, Bayleef, and&nbsp;
of course, Pikachu. Pikachu has expressed a lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:25.980
of interest in evolving, but that doesn't mean&nbsp;
Ash can't evolve his other Pokémon to make them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.480
stronger. While many people who play the video&nbsp;
games tend to evolve their Pokémon pretty quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.100
Ash doesn’t seem to mind keeping his companions&nbsp;
in their smaller, less powerful forms.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:40.440
In the Pokémon universe, every species has&nbsp;
an extremely rare color variant known as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.780
shiny form. For instance, a shiny Gyarados is&nbsp;
mostly red instead of its usual blue color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.360
while a shiny Psyduck is blue instead of yellow.&nbsp;
Many shiny Pokémon are noticeably different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.580
their normal forms, but some are pretty difficult&nbsp;
to identify. Some particularly bad offenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.660
like shiny Pikachu and shiny Gengar, are&nbsp;
just slightly different shades of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:06.120
normal colors. Ash's one shiny Pokémon, Noctowl,&nbsp;
falls somewhere between noticeable and subtle.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:11.160
At first glance, Ash's Noctowl can probably&nbsp;
fool anyone who isn't familiar with the species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.720
since the shiny variant and normal variant&nbsp;
both have brown color schemes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.420
when Ash's Noctowl is side by side with a normal&nbsp;
Noctowl, the difference is clear. Ash's Noctowl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:26.700
has a much lighter body and copper-toned wings,&nbsp;
while the normal Noctowl is darker overall.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.560
Since his tenth birthday, Ash has put&nbsp;
all of his time and effort into becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:35.760
the best Pokémon trainer he can be. He&nbsp;
focuses heavily on training and battling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.720
and doesn't usually show much interest in&nbsp;
anything that isn't related to Pokémon...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:45.000
including romance. But despite Ash's lack of&nbsp;
interest and experience in the love department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.700
a few characters have taken an interest in him.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:52.800
As Ash travels through the Kalos region, he's&nbsp;
accompanied by his childhood friend Serena. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.020
revealed in Episodes 6 and 7 of "Pokémon the&nbsp;
Series: XY" that the two of them met at camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.860
where Ash helped and encouraged Serena&nbsp;
during a difficult time. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.520
he's held a special place in her heart&nbsp;
and has been a source of inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.300
for her — although she hasn’t&nbsp;
exactly had the same effect on him.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:16.200
"Hmmm… Can’t say I remember you…"
"Aw?! Oh nooo…"

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.960
Serena gives Ash a kiss before parting ways with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.440
him in one of the most unexpected&nbsp;
moments in "Pokémon" anime history.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:27.000
In addition to Serena, Ash has won over&nbsp;
the heart of one Pokémon in particular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.200
Chikorita might have acted stubborn and&nbsp;
tough in the beginning, but the grass-type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.640
Pokémon is ultimately swayed by the boy's&nbsp;
kindness. She gives him a kiss on the cheek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.200
when he asks if she'd like to join him on his&nbsp;
adventure. Once Chikorita evolves into Bayleef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.680
she develops a habit of tackling her&nbsp;
trainer out of love and excitement.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.700
The "Pokémon" anime wouldn't be&nbsp;
nearly as exciting without some chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.140
but it’s not just Team Rocket that’s causing&nbsp;
trouble. Throughout Ash’s many adventures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:57.420
mischievous Pokémon seem to be naturally attracted&nbsp;
to him. Whether these troublemakers are causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:02.100
problems around town or just messing with their&nbsp;
trainers, it's always up to Ash to handle it.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.000
There are two notable instances where&nbsp;
Ash befriends and captures the leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.500
of a villainous Pokémon group. The first is&nbsp;
the infamous Squirtle Squad that initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.720
helps Team Rocket kidnap Pikachu in Episode 12.&nbsp;
After realizing who the bad guys really are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.280
the Squad works with Ash to defeat Team&nbsp;
Rocket and put out a forest fire. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.880
leader joins Ash's team while the rest&nbsp;
of the Squirtles become firefighters.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.180
A similar group of Sandile&nbsp;
appears in Episode 3 of "Pokémon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.960
Black &amp; White," where they act out in&nbsp;
an attempt to get everyone away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.400
dangerous geysers. Ash eventually sees their&nbsp;
good intentions, and they all work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:39.600
to get everyone to safety. The leader of&nbsp;
the pack follows Ash around in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.220
episodes before officially being caught&nbsp;
later on after evolving into Krokorok.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:49.500
Despite remaining 10 years old throughout the&nbsp;
many seasons of the anime, Ash's appearance has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:54.240
definitely undergone some changes. Ash always&nbsp;
looks the same throughout the Kanto and Johto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.520
eras, but alterations after that mostly come&nbsp;
down to new clothes, hats, and an upgraded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.440
Pokédex. Those changes in appearance are fairly&nbsp;
subtle up until the series "Pokémon: Sun &amp; Moon."

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.840
When Ash arrives in the Alola Region, more than&nbsp;
just his outfit gets a makeover. There's a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.220
shift in art style, which results in Ash&nbsp;
having an overall younger appearance. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.960
even ditches the fingerless gloves previously&nbsp;
considered a key part of his outfits. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.100
in "Pokémon Journeys," Ash gets bigger&nbsp;
irises in his eyes to make him look even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.880
more youthful. Perhaps the only physical&nbsp;
features that always stay the same for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:31.680
Ash are the zig-zag markings on his cheeks&nbsp;
that look like Pikachu’s lightning bolts.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:37.260
After traveling through region after region, Ash&nbsp;
finally snags his first championship title in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:43.830
Manalo Conference of the Alola Region. In Episodes&nbsp;
46 and 47 of "Pokémon the Series: Sun &amp; Moon —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:49.620
Ultra Legends," Ash's final battle against his&nbsp;
rival Gladion starts out pretty balanced. Ash's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:54.840
Melmetal is taken down by Gladion's Silvally,&nbsp;
who is then knocked out by Pikachu. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.120
Pikachu faces down Gladion's Zoroark, and they&nbsp;
both use their Z-Moves to take each other out.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:04.440
This leaves Gladion and Ash with just&nbsp;
one Pokémon each. Coincidence or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.620
they both put their faith in&nbsp;
their two Lycanroc. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:11.940
it doesn't look good for Ash's Lycanroc as it&nbsp;
struggles to deal with the other Lycanroc's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:16.800
counter-attack. But thankfully, Ash comes up with&nbsp;
an unconventional strategy to secure the victory.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:20.760
"Now, use counter against counter!"
"No way!"

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:24.360
This gives Ash's Lycanroc enough&nbsp;
momentum to deal the final blow.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:29.100
While it's exciting to watch Ash's strategy&nbsp;
play out, the win isn't as satisfying as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.180
it could have been. In the video games, a&nbsp;
counter cannot actually be countered itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:38.160
making Ash's use of it not feel totally&nbsp;
legit. This isn't the only time the anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.640
breaks the rules that the video games&nbsp;
have established, but it's a shame that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.180
it happens in the all-important battle&nbsp;
that crowns Ash as the new champion.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.460
While Ash may not have the best track record when&nbsp;
it comes to putting together a solid team of six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:55.500
his squad in "Pokémon Master Journeys" is&nbsp;
undeniably powerful — featuring Pikachu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:01.560
Lucario, Gengar, Dragonite, Dracovish,&nbsp;
and Sirfetch'd. Surprisingly, all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.400
Pokémon are in their final evolutions, with&nbsp;
the exception of Pikachu. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:10.620
it works out for Pikachu, as the electric mouse&nbsp;
has a special Gigantamax form that Raichu doesn’t.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:16.500
Aside from Pikachu, Ash has two of the strongest&nbsp;
Pokémon from Kanto in his corner — Gengar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:21.660
Dragonite. Gengar is a speedy glass cannon&nbsp;
and another potential Gigantamax option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:27.120
while Dragonite is a bulky and reliable flying&nbsp;
dragon. Ash also has a great Mega Evolution in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:32.340
Lucario. This Lucario has shown the most growth on&nbsp;
the team by starting out as an egg in Ash's care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:38.160
training hard as a little Riolu, and evolving&nbsp;
into a total powerhouse. The Galar duo — Dracovish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:43.260
and Sirfetch'd — should not be underestimated&nbsp;
either, despite their somewhat silly designs.

